# mojo-dllm maintainer profile.
#
# Every site-specific value lives here. lib/run.sh reads it and stays generic,
# so a second repository needs a second profile and no code change.
#
# Generated by ./new-profile.sh. Read it once end to end before the first run:
# this file decides what an unattended agent does in your name.

PROFILE_NAME="mojo-dllm"
# A dedicated review checkout this profile owns, cloned from REPO_ORIGIN by run.sh when
# it is missing and kept on origin/main. It has no Mojo toolchain and never builds.
REPO_PATH="${MAINTAINER_REPO_PATH:-$HOME/.local/state/mojo-dllm-maint/checkout}"
REPO_ORIGIN="${MAINTAINER_REPO_ORIGIN:-https://github.com/dllm-labs/mojo-dllm.git}"
REPO_SLUG="dllm-labs/mojo-dllm"

# The name the agent uses for you in a comment, and the account that must own
# every write. run.sh reads the login back and refuses to start as anyone else,
# because a write that SUCCEEDS under the wrong account is worse than a 403.
MAINTAINER_NAME="Vladimir"
GH_ACCOUNT="vladimirrott"

STATE_DIR="${MAINTAINER_STATE_DIR:-$HOME/.local/state/mojo-dllm-maint}"
HELPER="$HOME/.local/bin/maintainer"

# Tasks this profile runs, and the model each gets.
TASKS="review issues"
# Thinking budget per task, in tokens, exported by the claude backend as
# MAX_THINKING_TOKENS. Reasoning effort is not a CLI flag; this is the
# mechanism. It costs money on every run, so let the task that reads diffs think
# and keep the sweeps cheap. Delete these lines to use the model's default.
THINKING_review=31999
THINKING_issues=16000

# Optional, per task: the model this task's subagents get, exported as
# CLAUDE_CODE_SUBAGENT_MODEL. Declare it only for a task that actually fans out.
# It is a default, so an agent definition with its own `model:` still wins.
# Requires Claude Code 2.1.238 or later.
SUBAGENT_MODEL_review="sonnet"
MODEL_review="opus"
MODEL_issues="opus"

# Minimum hours between two runs of the same task, enforced by run.sh rather
# than by the scheduler. launchd cannot express "every N days" at all, and
# cron's day-of-month stepping fires on the 31st and again on the 1st. Keep
# these below the nominal interval so a jittered run is never suppressed.
MIN_HOURS_review=6
MIN_HOURS_issues=36

# on | off. ON since 2026-11-27, at Vladimir's instruction, with the same settings
# as the sysknife profile. It started at off: the first run read the open pull
# requests, found his review already at the head and drafted nothing new. The
# project's AGENTS.md lets an agent post only when the person it works for asks, and
# he asked. Merge, push, release and tag stay denied by the wall regardless of POST.
POST="${MAINTAINER_POST:-on}"

# Prose discipline, applied to everything the agent publishes. Opt-OUT: a run
# gets it unless someone deliberately sets "raw", which exists for debugging a
# prompt rather than for shipping.
PROSE_STYLE="${MAINTAINER_PROSE_STYLE:-stop-slop}"

# claude | opencode | codex | cursor. See the README for what each can contain.
# Only claude and opencode can honour POST=off.
BACKEND="${MAINTAINER_BACKEND:-claude}"

# Tunables with a default (LOCK_WAIT, RETENTION_DAYS, VERIFY_MEMORY and the
# rest) live in lib/defaults.env and are documented in docs/configuration.md.
# Set one here only to change it for this profile, e.g. RETENTION_DAYS=30.

# Finishing stalled pull requests (`maintainer stalled`, `maintainer-repo fixup`).
# The quiet window, the heads-up window and the patch size cap have defaults in
# lib/defaults.env (FIXUP_QUIET_DAYS, FIXUP_NOTICE_DAYS, FIXUP_MAX_LINES); set one
# here only to change it for this repository. Left unset, a `test-count` item is
# refused. A command that prints the repository's test count and nothing else,
# run over a contributor's tree, so it must COUNT (grep, find, wc) and never
# build or run their tests:
# FIXUP_COUNT_CMD="grep -rc '#\[test\]' src | awk -F: '{n+=$2} END {print n}'"

# The label this project puts on an issue somebody has claimed. The merge gate
# refuses a pull request that closes a claimed issue its author never posted on,
# because the label is a public promise other contributors read and stay off.
# Empty disables the check, for a project that does not work this way. This one holds an
# issue with a `for:<handle>` label instead, so the check stays off.
CLAIM_LABEL=""

# Which green Dependabot bumps `maintainer deps` sends to `verify` (none, patch or
# minor), as in the sysknife profile. The review prompt holds back a bump of the
# Mojo toolchain: the project's CONTRIBUTING.md says those stand alone.
DEPS_AUTOMERGE="minor"

# Commands the prompts tell the agent to run. maintainer-doctor checks each one
# resolves; the test suite refuses a prompt that names a hyphenated word outside
# this list plus KNOWN_NAMES. A prompt once told a run to call a command that
# had been renamed, and the run reported a verdict it could not have obtained.
REQUIRED_COMMANDS="maintainer maintainer-merge maintainer-repo gh git"

# Hyphenated names in the prompts that are NOT commands: skills, labels, crates.
KNOWN_NAMES="stop-slop"

# Paths whose change invalidates a verification receipt, and which count as
# production for `maintainer-repo release-check`. Tests, docs and evidence
# artifacts may move between the head a receipt names and the head being merged;
# these may not. There is no default: a merge gate that guesses which paths are
# production will guess wrong on somebody's repository.
PROD_GLOBS="src/* launcher/* scripts/* bench/* .github/* pyproject.toml uv.lock mojo-dllm"
# What a release actually publishes. PROD_GLOBS decides whether a receipt still
# holds and therefore includes CI; this set decides whether a release reaches
# anybody, and CI does not. `maintainer-repo release-check` prints both, because
# a CHANGELOG entry saying a gate "refuses" reads as a break either way and only
# one of the two is a break in the product.
SHIPPED_GLOBS="src/* launcher/* scripts/* pyproject.toml uv.lock mojo-dllm"
